Would it make sense to `return this` in `reply.send`?
In this way also the following case is fully supported:
```js
async function handler (req, reply) {
  const someResult = await anAsyncFunction(req.body)
  if (someResult.error) {
    return reply.code(500).send({ error: someResult.error })
  }

  return reply.send({ success: 'is-you' })
}
```

comments:
I am a strong 👎 on removing support for:

```js
fastify.get('/', async function (req, reply) {
  // do some promise stuff
  // ...
  reply.send({hello: 'world'})
})
```

The only reason I use `async` handlers is to work with promise based functions before issuing the reply.

@jsumners if you use only promises you don't need `async/await`

```js
fastify.get('/', function (req, reply) {
  // do some promise stuff
  // ...
  return Promise.resolve()
})
```

if you use `async` you should use the corresponding `await` style
```js
fastify.get('/', async function (req, reply) {
  // do some await stuff
  // ...
  return value;
})
```

if you use `callback-style` you can just use `.send`
```js
fastify.get('/', function (req, reply) {
  // do some callback stuff
  // ...
  reply.send({hello: 'world'})
})
```

but as soon as you try to mix different styles it gets hairy very fast and this is what I'm talking about. We even recommend/support different styles and we should do the opposite.

@jsumners removing support for `reply.send` is not my intention. I'd like to start a discussion because a combination of callback and async/await has lead to confusions in the community.

> The async operations have nothing to do with sending the response, only building the response. I’m not listing a complete example here because it is beside the point. Which is: reply.send should work regardless of the function declaration.

I don't know if I understood you correctly but with the use of `async/await` or `promises` the control flow should be stay in the promise chain not by a callback. That's how it should work.

```js
async function handler (req, reply) {
  const someResult = await anAsyncFunction(req.body)
  if (someResult.error) {
    return reply.code(500).send({error: someResult.error})
  }

  reply.send({success: 'is-you'})
}
```

That's the simplest I can make it. *There are use cases* for using `reply.send` in an `async` function. Simply returning the resolution, or rejection, of a promise *is not sufficient* in all cases.

I think using `reply.send()` within async functions is a pretty solid case, however I agree that the developer experience it's currently not there yet.

How about we support:

```js
fastify
  .get('/', async (request, reply) => {
    someAsync(() => reply.send())
    await reply
  });
```

This would be possible by making reply a thenable. This would be possible just by implenting [`then`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise/then)

A bit late to the party and maybe not posting in the correct place, but I came across this situation today. My endpoint looks kind of like this,

```javascript
async function getOtp(
	request: FastifyRequestTypebox<typeof LoginSchema>,
	reply: FastifyReplyTypebox<typeof LoginSchema>,
) => {
        // do async/await validation, db queries, etc
        reply.send();
	await sendOtpEmail({ id: 1234 });
},
```
The email client I'm using takes on average about 4-5 seconds to resolve successfully. It's not terribly long, but I've decided to just send the email after calling `reply.send`, so that the user doesn't see a full screen spinner during the time they wait.

Anyway, I presume that this isn't possible to do?

@mkhoussid, I solved my similar problem by doing this:

```ts
async function getOtp(request, reply) {
  await reply.send();
  await sendOtpEmail({ id: 1234 });
}
```
